      "Self-propulsion exists to solve two problems: physical effort and control on uneven ground, and the Honda HRX217VKA solves both better than anything else we tested. Its Versamow system lets the drive speed be adjusted independently from the engine throttle via a single dial, so you can slow the wheels down for careful trimming near beds while keeping the blade spinning at full cutting speed, or open the drive up to nearly running speed on open, flat stretches. Rear-wheel drive, rather than the front-wheel drive common on cheaper self-propelled mowers, was the standout difference in our hill testing — on a moderately sloped 15-degree section of our test yard, the HRX217VKA maintained consistent traction and never spun a wheel, while several front-wheel-drive competitors struggled to pull themselves uphill without the operator pushing. The GCV200 engine, built in-house by Honda rather than licensed, started on the first pull in every test session across a full season, including after winter storage. The twin-blade MicroCut system mulches clippings into a fine enough texture that they disappear into the lawn without any visible clumping. All of this comes at a real price — this is a $700+ mower — but the build quality, engine reliability, and genuinely superior hill performance justify it for anyone who plans to keep a mower for over a decade.",
  },
  {
