      "Self-propulsion has historically carried a real price premium, and the Craftsman M275 exists to close that gap for budget-conscious buyers. At a price that undercuts most self-propelled competitors by hundreds of dollars, it delivers the core benefit — not having to physically push the mower — without much else in the way of refinement. The 159cc engine handled our test yard's flat and mildly sloped sections without issue, though it showed more strain than Honda's engine when pushed through the thickest, wettest section of test grass. Front-wheel drive keeps the design simple and genuinely easy to learn; first-time self-propelled mower owners in our informal testing group picked up the controls faster on the Craftsman than on Toro's speed-sensing system, simply because there's less to think about. That simplicity comes with the expected trade-off on hills — the front wheels lost traction noticeably on our steepest test section, requiring the operator to help push through. Build quality is the other honest compromise: plastic components feel less substantial than Honda's or Toro's, and long-term durability reviews suggest this isn't a mower built to last fifteen years the way a premium option might. For a flat-to-mildly-sloped yard on a real budget, it's a legitimately good value.",
  },
  {
    id: "ryobi-40v-hp-brushless-ry401170",
    name: "Ryobi 40V HP Brushless (RY401170)",
    category: "Battery, Front-Wheel Drive, Variable Speed",
    badge: "Best Value Battery Self-Propelled",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6800,
    pros: [
      "Brushless motor delivers surprisingly strong torque for the price tier",
      "Compatible with Ryobi's broader 40V battery ecosystem, useful if you already own other tools",
      "Variable-speed front-wheel drive is smooth and responsive in testing",
      "LED headlights are a genuinely useful touch for early morning or evening mowing",
      "Noticeably cheaper than EGO's comparable self-propelled battery mower",
