    bottomLine:
      "The best value in battery-powered self-propulsion. Not quite EGO's equal on hills, but a genuinely strong performer for hundreds less.",
    description:
      "Ryobi has built a reputation for delivering a large percentage of premium-brand performance at a meaningfully lower price, and the RY401170 fits that pattern well. Its brushless motor produced more torque than we expected at this price point, cutting through moderately thick grass in our testing without the bogging that plagues cheaper battery mowers. The front-wheel-drive system uses a variable-speed trigger that felt smooth and predictable to operate, though as with any front-wheel-drive mower, it showed its limits on our steepest hill test, requiring more operator assistance than the rear-wheel-drive EGO or Honda to maintain a straight uphill line. Where the Ryobi has a genuine advantage is ecosystem compatibility — if you already own Ryobi 40V string trimmers, blowers, or other outdoor tools, the batteries are interchangeable, which meaningfully reduces the real cost of adding this mower to your setup. The LED headlights built into the front of the deck turned out to be more useful than we expected, letting us finish a mow in fading evening light without needing to rush. Runtime and cutting performance both dipped more noticeably than premium competitors when grass was thick and damp, which is the clearest sign of where the cost savings come from. For anyone already in the Ryobi ecosystem or working with a tighter budget, it's a smart buy.",
  },
  {
    id: "snapper-sp80-82v",
    name: "Snapper SP80 82V Self-Propelled",
    category: "Battery, Rear-Wheel Drive, High-Torque",
    badge: "Best for Hills",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 3100,
    pros: [
      "82V battery platform delivers the highest torque of any battery mower we tested",
      "Rear-wheel drive with aggressive tread pattern provides the best hill traction in this category",
      "21-inch deck with 7 cutting-height positions covers a wide range of grass types",
      "Push-button start with no pull cord",
      "Handles thick, tall, or slightly overgrown grass better than most battery competitors",
    ],
    cons: [
      "82V battery and charger are proprietary and pricier to replace than more common platforms",
      "Heavier than most battery mowers due to the larger battery pack",
