        <!-- SECTION 3: Drive Types -->
        <div id="drive-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Front-Wheel Drive vs Rear-Wheel Drive Self-Propelled Mowers
          </h2>

          <p>
            This single spec matters more than almost any other feature on a self-propelled mower, and it's the one most first-time buyers overlook.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Front-Wheel Drive</h3>
          <p>
            Front-wheel-drive mowers are generally simpler and less expensive, and they make it easy to lift the front end to pivot around obstacles, which many owners find intuitive. The trade-off is traction: as the mower's weight naturally shifts backward while being pushed, the front wheels — carrying less weight — are more prone to slipping, particularly on hills, wet grass, or thick patches. Front-wheel drive is a genuinely good fit for flat to gently sloped yards.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rear-Wheel Drive</h3>
          <p>
            Rear-wheel-drive mowers put the drive wheels where the mower's weight naturally concentrates during use, which translates directly into better traction and pulling power, especially on hills and uneven terrain. In our hill testing, every rear-wheel-drive mower on this list climbed our test slope without assistance, while front-wheel-drive competitors required the operator to help push. Rear-wheel drive mowers tend to cost more and are slightly less nimble for tight pivoting, but for any yard with real elevation change, the traction advantage is worth it. If you're not sure which category your yard falls into, walk the steepest section and see whether you naturally slow down or lean forward to keep your footing — if you do, your lawn is sloped enough to benefit from rear-wheel drive.
          </p>

