        <!-- SECTION 4: Gas vs Battery -->
        <div id="gas-vs-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gas vs Battery Self-Propelled Mowers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The power-source decision matters more for self-propelled mowers than for push mowers, because drive strength — not just blade power — depends directly on the engine or motor.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gas self-propelled mowers</strong> still hold a slight edge in raw, sustained pulling power, particularly on large properties where a mower needs to run continuously for over an hour without interruption. Honda's rear-wheel-drive engines remain the benchmark for combining strong drive torque with reliable starting. The downsides are the same as always: noise, fumes, fuel and oil management, and more required maintenance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery self-propelled mowers</strong> have closed the performance gap dramatically in the last several product generations, particularly at higher voltages like EGO's 56V and Snapper's 82V platforms. For lawns up to roughly half an acre, a good battery self-propelled mower now delivers drive strength and cutting power that's genuinely comparable to mid-tier gas, with the added benefits of near-silent operation, no fuel management, and dramatically less routine maintenance. The remaining trade-off is runtime — very large properties or back-to-back mowing sessions still favor gas's unlimited fuel-tank runtime over a battery's finite charge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Choosing Speed & Terrain -->
        <div id="choosing-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Self-Propelled Mowers for Hills: Matching Drive and Speed to Your Terrain
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flat Yards Under Half an Acre</h3>
          <p>
            Front-wheel drive is genuinely sufficient here, and the money saved versus a rear-wheel-drive model is better spent elsewhere — a wider deck, a stronger engine, or simply a lower total price. Variable speed is a nice convenience but not essential on flat ground where a fixed pace works fine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Yards With Moderate Slopes</h3>
          <p>
            This is the threshold where rear-wheel drive starts to matter. Any consistent incline steep enough that you notice yourself working harder while pushing a mower uphill is steep enough to benefit from the traction advantage of rear-wheel drive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Steep Hills or Uneven Terrain</h3>
          <p>
            Rear-wheel drive isn't optional here — it's the difference between a mower that pulls itself up the hill and one that requires you to physically push it the entire way. Pair rear-wheel drive with a variable-speed system so you can slow the mower down on the steepest sections for better control.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Large Properties (Half Acre and Up)</h3>
          <p>
            Prioritize either a gas mower or a high-voltage battery platform with a spare battery on hand, since runtime becomes the limiting factor on large properties before drive strength does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Cost of Ownership -->
        <div id="cost-of-ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Self-Propelled Mower Cost of Ownership Over 5 Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            The sticker price on a self-propelled mower is only part of the real cost. Gas mowers carry ongoing expenses that battery mowers largely avoid, while battery mowers concentrate their cost upfront in the battery pack itself. Looking at a realistic five-year ownership window changes how the math looks compared to day-one pricing alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            A typical gas self-propelled mower running 25-30 mowing sessions per season will consume roughly $60-100 in fuel annually, plus $30-50 per year in oil, spark plugs, and air filters, plus an occasional $80-150 tune-up or belt replacement every few years. Over five years, that routine upkeep can add $600-900 on top of the mower's purchase price — a figure many buyers don't account for when comparing sticker prices at the store.
          </p>

          <p>
            A battery self-propelled mower has almost none of that ongoing cost. Electricity to recharge a typical mower battery costs a few dollars per season at most, and there's no oil, filters, or spark plugs to replace. The real long-term cost is battery degradation — most quality lithium-ion mower batteries are rated for several hundred charge cycles and hold up well for 5+ years of typical residential use, but a replacement battery, when eventually needed, can run $150-300 depending on the platform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded electric and reel push mowers remain the cheapest to own over any time horizon among mowers generally, but they're rarely offered in self-propelled form since the whole point of a cord-free or motor-free design conflicts with adding a drive system. Among genuinely self-propelled options, a battery mower typically comes out ahead of a comparable gas mower in total five-year cost once fuel, oil, and routine maintenance are factored in, even though the gas mower often looks cheaper on the shelf.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Maintenance -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Self-Propelled Mower Maintenance and Common Repairs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Self-propelled mowers have one additional maintenance point that push mowers don't: the drive system itself, typically a belt or cable connecting the engine or motor to the wheels.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Most Common Self-Propelled Mower Problem</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              If a self-propelled mower stops driving itself but the engine or motor still runs and the blade still spins, the most likely cause is a stretched or broken drive cable, or a worn drive belt. Both are inexpensive, commonly available replacement parts and a manageable weekend repair on most models — check your owner's manual before assuming you need a whole new mower.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Beyond the drive system, maintenance largely mirrors any mower of the same power type. Gas self-propelled mowers need seasonal oil changes, air filter checks, and spark plug replacement, plus periodic inspection of the drive belt for wear or fraying. Battery self-propelled mowers need almost none of that — mainly keeping the underside of the deck clean of grass buildup, which can restrict wheel movement over time, and storing the battery at a moderate charge level during winter. Across both power types, keeping the wheels and drive gears free of grass clippings and debris after each mow meaningfully extends the life of the drive system.
          </p>

          <p>
            It's also worth inspecting the drive wheels themselves at least once a season. Worn or cracked drive tires lose traction long before they look obviously damaged, and on a rear-wheel-drive mower relied on for hill traction, a worn tire can quietly undo much of the advantage the drive system is supposed to provide. Replacement drive wheels are inexpensive and widely available for most major brands, and swapping them is a straightforward task that doesn't require a full service visit.
          </p>
        </div>

