            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best self-propelled lawn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, the Honda HRX217VKA offers the best overall combination of drive strength, hill performance, and long-term reliability. For a quieter, battery-powered option with comparable drive performance, the EGO Power+ LM2135SP is the top pick. For yards with genuine hills, the Snapper SP80 82V delivers the strongest traction of any battery mower tested.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is front-wheel drive or rear-wheel drive better for a self-propelled mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rear-wheel drive provides significantly better traction on hills and uneven terrain because the mower's weight naturally shifts backward while pushing, increasing grip on the drive wheels. Front-wheel drive is simpler, often cheaper, and performs fine on flat or gently sloped yards, but loses traction more readily on real inclines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are battery self-propelled mowers as powerful as gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Modern high-voltage battery mowers, particularly 56V and 80V+ platforms, now deliver drive torque and cutting power comparable to mid-tier gas mowers for lawns up to roughly half an acre. Gas retains an edge in unlimited runtime and typically slightly stronger performance in extremely thick or tall grass, but the practical gap has narrowed significantly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a self-propelled mower for a small, flat yard?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily. Self-propulsion adds the most value on larger lawns, hilly or uneven terrain, and for anyone with physical limitations that make pushing a mower difficult. On a small, flat yard under a quarter acre, a lighter push mower is often easier to maneuver around obstacles and cheaper to buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does variable-speed drive work on a self-propelled mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Variable-speed drive lets you adjust how fast the wheels pull the mower forward independent of the engine or motor speed, typically via a dial or a walk-sensing system like Toro's Personal Pace. This lets you slow down for careful maneuvering around obstacles while keeping full cutting power, then speed up on open stretches to finish faster.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a good self-propelled lawn mower cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Budget gas self-propelled mowers start around $300-350, mid-range gas and value battery mowers run $400-550, and premium gas or battery models with rear-wheel drive and variable speed typically cost $600-900 including a battery and charger where applicable.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes When Buying a Self-Propelled Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Self-propulsion is one of the easier upgrades to get wrong, mostly because the drive system's real-world impact isn't obvious from a spec sheet alone.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Drive Type for a Sloped Yard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The single most common mistake is buying a front-wheel-drive mower for a yard with real elevation change, then being surprised when it struggles on hills. If your yard has any noticeable slope, prioritize rear-wheel drive even if it costs more — it's the spec that determines whether self-propulsion actually delivers on its promise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Battery Without Checking Runtime Against Yard Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A battery mower rated for 45-60 minutes of runtime is genuinely fine for a quarter to half-acre lawn, but will leave you swapping batteries mid-mow on a larger property. Measure your actual lawn size and compare it honestly against the manufacturer's runtime estimate before assuming one battery will be enough.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Neglecting the Drive Belt or Cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The drive mechanism is the one component a push mower never has to deal with, and it's easy to forget it needs attention. A neglected, grass-clogged drive system wears out faster and can eventually stop the wheels from turning entirely, even though the engine or motor and blade still work fine — leading owners to wrongly assume the whole mower has failed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overpaying for Self-Propulsion on a Genuinely Small, Flat Yard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Self-propulsion adds real cost and weight, and on a small, flat lawn under a quarter acre, that added weight can actually make the mower harder to maneuver around tight obstacles than a light push mower would be. Be honest about whether your yard size and terrain actually justify the upgrade before spending the extra money.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

